  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA weak area of low pressure is beginning to track eastward\nacross the region which is currently located across the western\nMohawk Valley. An area of showers are accompanying this low and\nare approaching the Hudson Valley and points east after passing\nthrough areas to the west this morning. No thunder has occurred\nwith this activity thus far and no thunder is expected to\ndevelop through the rest of the day. These showers will produce\nanywhere from 0.25 to 0.75 inches in some areas through this \nevening but will not result in any hydrological impacts. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA cold front crosses the area later tonight as surface high\npressure builds in from Canada. Prior to the frontal passage,\nsome low clouds and/or fog is likely with low level moisture\nlingering. As drier air begins to filter in later tonight, these\nclouds and fog should begin to dissipate. Sunshine returns for\nFriday along with decreasing humidity. There will be a light\nbreeze out of the northwest where some gusts to around 20-25 \nmph will be possible during the afternoon hours. Highs will \nreach the upper 60s to upper 70s. A weak upper level shortwave \nwill cross the region Friday evening which will bring a brief \nperiod of high clouds, though with the dry air mass in place, \ntemperatures will still be able to fall quickly. Most areas will\nfall back into the 40s with some mid to upper 30s in the higher\nelevations. Some patchy frost remains possible across parts of \nthe Adirondacks, though overall coverage may not be enough to \nwarrant an advisory. Another mostly sunny and dry day is \nexpected Saturday but with continued cooling aloft, it will not \nbe as warm as on Friday with most areas only reaching the 60s.\n\nGuidance continues to be consistent with a low pressure system\ntracking south of our area (likely across northern PA) with\nfavorable upper level support and isentropic lift to bring a\nwidespread rainfall to the region. Rain will begin toward\ndaybreak Sunday and continue throughout the day before tapering\noff Sunday evening and night. The heaviest rainfall totals will\nlikely occur across southern areas which will be within the axis\nof greatest moisture (precipitable water values greater than\n1.50 inches) and forcing for ascent. Current expected rainfall\namounts are around 1 to 2 inches from Albany and points south\nwith 0.25 inches to 1.00 inches to the north. Most of this rain\nshould be beneficial due to the prolonged period of\nprecipitation but will monitor for some localized ponding water\nin urban and poor drainage areas should some heavier periods of\nrain occur, especially for southern areas. Temperatures may not\nget out of the 50s for most areas on Sunday.\n\nWe continue to be in close proximity to a stalled frontal \nboundary to our south for early next week. High pressure will \nattempt to nose in from the north. Where the front sets up will \ndetermine when chances for precipitation return. Right now, the \nhigh looks to win out on most days bringing mainly dry weather, \nthough if the front positions itself slightly farther north, \nthe chances for some showers on some days will increase. Will \ncontinue to monitor trends. Regardless, temperatures look to run\nslightly below normal during this time with highs mainly in the\n60s with some 50s in the higher elevations.",
